    <DataNode elementId="AT2G45300-MONOMER" textLabel="3-phosphoshikimate 1-carboxyvinyltransferase, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="P05466" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The gene encoding 5-enolpyruvylshikimate-3-phosphate synthase has been identified and partially characterized in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. Its molecular function to catalyze the condensation of phosphoenolpyruvate with shikimate 3-phosphate to yield 5-enolpyruvylshikimate 3-phosphate (EPSP) has been shown by glyphosate tolerance gained in Arabidopsis through overexpression of the gene |CITS:[3481024]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G45300-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G45300.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="P05466" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="CAA29828" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="5-enolpyruvylshikimate-3-phosphate synthase" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="EPSP synthase" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-5099" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G45300.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G45300" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_3481024" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT2G21940-MONOMER" textLabel="Shikimate kinase 1, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SJ05" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, dreher">SK1 is one of two shikimate kinases from |FRAME: ORG-5993 Arabidopsis thaliana| that phosphorylate |FRAME: SHIKIMATE shikimate|. As part of the shikimate pathway, these enzymes help to move carbon from primary metabolism to a broad range of secondary metabolites important for plant growth, development, and survival under stress |CITS: [19057671]|. Although they have relatively similar enzymatic activities, |FRAME: AT2G21940-MONOMER SK1| and |FRAME: AT4G39540-MONOMER SK2| expression patterns are different with |FRAME: AT2G21940-MONOMER SK1| transcripts being expressed most highly in mature embryos and senescing leaves while |FRAME: AT4G39540-MONOMER SK2| transcripts are expressed throughout vegetative development and early on during embryogenesis. In addition, |FRAME: AT2G21940-MONOMER SK1| transcripts rise during heat stress and recovery, whereas |FRAME: AT4G39540-MONOMER SK2| has higher levels of transcripts following innoculation with spores of |FRAME: TAX-4787 &lt;i&gt;Phytophthora infestans&lt;/i&gt;| |CITS: [19057671]|. It is believed that this protein is found in the chloroplast based on the presence of a putative N-terminal transit peptide and based on the observation that shikimate kinases from spinach and tomato are targeted to the chloroplast |CITS: [16667533][1338949][19057671]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G21940-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G21940.4" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SJ05" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="AtSK1" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-18397" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G21940.4" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G21940" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_19057671" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT4G33510-MONOMER" textLabel="Phospho-2-dehydro-3-deoxyheptonate aldolase 2, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT4G33510.1" dataSource="TAIR gene name" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The cDNA encoding 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 7-phosphate synthase (DHS2) was identified and isolated from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[1681544]|. The molecular function was confirmed by functional complementation of DHS deficient yeast mutants |CITS:[1681544]|. The enzyme catalyzes the first committed step in the biosynthesis of aromatic amino acids, i.e. the condensation of phosphoenolpyruvate and erythrose 4-phosphate to yield 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 7-phosphate (DAHP). In contrast to the other gene encoding 3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 7-phosphate synthase in Arabidopsis DHS1, DHS2 was not induced in response to wounding and bacterial pathogens (&lt;i&gt;Pseudomonas syringae&lt;/i&gt;) indicating distinct physiological roles.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT4G33510-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT4G33510.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pid" value="AAA32785" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="3-deoxy-D-arabino-heptulosonate 7-phosphate synthase 2" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="DAHP synthase 2" />
      <Property key="Synonym_3" value="Phospho-2-keto-3-deoxyheptonate aldolase 2" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-10447" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT4G33510.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT4G33510" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_1681544" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT3G06350-MONOMER" textLabel="Bifunctional 3-dehydroquinate dehydratase/shikimate dehydrogenase, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SQT8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">DHQ-SDH is a multifunctional enzyme. The enzyme atalyzes two consecutive reactions in the shikimate biosynthesis pathway, transforming |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE 3-dehydroquinate| (DQ) to |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE 3-dehydroshikimate| (DHS) and then to |FRAME: SHIKIMATE shikimate| |CITS: [16784230]|. In addition, the enzyme can catalyze the oxidation of |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE 3-dehydroshikimate| to |FRAME: CPD-13085 3,5-didehydroshikimate|, which immidiately and spontaneously rearranges to form |FRAME: CPD-183 gallate| |CITS: [Muir11]|. The enzyme has been co-crystalized with a few different substrates to elucidate its structure and bifunctional behavior |CITS: [16784230][SINGH07]|. These studies reveal that the enzyme has a concave architecture that places the active sites for the two different enzymatic activities in a face-to-face arrangement. Two compounds associated with the first reaction |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE DQ| and |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE DHS| can be siphoned off into quinate metabolic pathways. While quinates may act as important anti-herbivory and cell wall precursor compounds, it is likley important for plants to maintain a constant flux through the shikimate pathway. The bifunctional nature of this enzyme may promote that outcome by creating a higher effective concentration of |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE DHS| at the shikimate dehydrogenase active site, thus reducing the tendency for |FRAME: 3-DEHYDRO-SHIKIMATE DHS| to exit the chorismate biosynthetic pathway |CITS: [16784230]|. A series of single amino-acid change shikimate dehydrogenase active site mutants were tested &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; to better understand this phenomenon |CITS: [16784230][SINGH07]|. The dehydroquinate dehydratase activity was not extensively characterized, but, when |FRAME: DEHYDROQUINATE DQ| was added to the DHQ-SDH crystal, it led to the formation of a Lys-241-dehydroshikimate Schiff base in the dehydroquinate dehydratase active site |CITS: [SINGH07]|. This protein is localized to the cholorplast |CITS: [18633119]| and may be present in the stroma |CITS: [18431481]|. Studies on DHQ-SDH were performed using a truncated version of the protein lacking the first 88-amino acids of the full-length protein. They are predicted to serve as the N-terminal transit peptide directing this protein to the chloroplast |CITS: [16784230][SINGH07]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G06350-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G06350.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9SQT8" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="DHQ-SDH protein" />
      <Property key="Synonym_2" value="DHQase-SORase" />
      <Property key="Synonym_3" value="Protein EMBRYO DEFECTIVE 3004[Includes: Dehydroquinate dehydratase" />
      <Property key="Synonym_4" value="DHQ" />
      <Property key="Synonym_5" value="SDH" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-18699, RXN-18400, RXN-18401" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="3" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT3G06350.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT3G06350" />
